--TEST--
Every `Core\Uuid` draw is canonical, distinct and round-trips through `parse`; `tryParse` agrees with `parse` on every spelling; and a refusal quotes the operand it refused, bounded — spec § 11, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4, ADR 0088
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A drawn UUID cannot be asserted, so the two generators are pinned by
// *invariance* over a run of draws rather than by any one value: each is
// counted only when it is canonical, carries its own version nibble, carries
// RFC 9562's variant, and comes back unchanged through `parse`. A generator
// that got any of those right most of the time would fail here.
string $seen = "";
int $repeats = 0;
int $draws = 0;

int $shape4 = 0;
int $trip4 = 0;
int $i = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 24; $i = $i + 1) {
    var $t = Core\Uuid::v4()->toString();
    $shape4 = $shape4
        + (Core\Regex::matches($t, '^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-4[0-9a-f]{3}-[89ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$') ? 1 : 0);
    // `toString` is the wire form, so `parse` of it is the identity — the
    // pair is a round trip and not two renderings that happen to agree.
    $trip4 = $trip4 + ((Core\Uuid::parse($t)->toString() == $t) ? 1 : 0);
    $repeats = $repeats + (Core\Str::contains($seen, $t) ? 1 : 0);
    $seen = $seen . $t . ";";
    $draws = $draws + 1;
}
echo "v4 ", $shape4, " ", $trip4, "\n";

// The same sweep for v7, whose version nibble is the only part of the
// rendering that differs from a v4's. Its millisecond clock is what makes it
// sort by creation time; the monotonic step across a *pause* is pinned by the
// unit test in `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/uuid.rs`, which can sleep.
int $shape7 = 0;
int $trip7 = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 24; $i = $i + 1) {
    // `$u`, not `$t` again: a `var` binding is function-scoped, so the second
    // loop cannot re-declare the first's name (`E0406`).
    var $u = Core\Uuid::v7()->toString();
    $shape7 = $shape7
        + (Core\Regex::matches($u, '^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-7[0-9a-f]{3}-[89ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$') ? 1 : 0);
    $trip7 = $trip7 + ((Core\Uuid::parse($u)->toString() == $u) ? 1 : 0);
    $repeats = $repeats + (Core\Str::contains($seen, $u) ? 1 : 0);
    $seen = $seen . $u . ";";
    $draws = $draws + 1;
}
echo "v7 ", $shape7, " ", $trip7, "\n";

// 48 draws, no value seen twice. A generator answering a constant, or one
// re-seeded per call, fails this line rather than the shape lines above.
echo "distinct ", $draws, " ", $repeats, "\n";

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4 and this module's own docs: `tryParse` is `parse` with the
// throw turned into a `null`, which is a claim about *every* spelling rather
// than about the rows each member's own case happens to write. So the two are
// swept together and only their agreement is counted.
array<string> $spellings = [
    "f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e4",
    "F9168C5E-CEB2-4FAA-B6BF-329BF39FA1E4",
    "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
    "f9168c5eceb24faab6bf329bf39fa1e4",
    "{f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e4}",
    "urn:uuid:f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e4",
    "f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e",
    "",
];
int $agree = 0;
int $accepted = 0;
foreach ($spellings as string $text) {
    var $maybe = Core\Uuid::tryParse($text);
    bool $threw = false;
    try {
        Core\Uuid::parse($text);
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        $threw = true;
    }
    $agree = $agree + ((($maybe != null) == !$threw) ? 1 : 0);
    $accepted = $accepted + (($maybe != null) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "agree ", $agree, " of ", Core\Arr::count($spellings), ", accepted ", $accepted, "\n";

// The refused operand is the one value in this member a caller sizes, and the
// message is written to a log — ADR 0088's wider rule — so the quote is
// bounded at 48 characters. The boundary is asserted on both sides and built
// with `Core\Str::repeat` rather than pasted: 48 is quoted whole, so the
// closing quote follows the last character, and 49 is cut to 48 with an
// ellipsis standing where the rest was.
try {
    Core\Uuid::parse(Core\Str::repeat("z", 48));
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo Core\Str::contains($e->message, "\"" . Core\Str::repeat("z", 48) . "\"") ? "whole" : "cut", "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Uuid::parse(Core\Str::repeat("z", 49));
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo Core\Str::contains($e->message, "\"" . Core\Str::repeat("z", 48) . "…\"") ? "bounded" : "unbounded", "\n";
}

// And the short case is quoted whole, which is what makes the message
// actionable at all — the near miss a program actually sends.
try {
    Core\Uuid::parse("f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e");
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
v4 24 24
v7 24 24
distinct 48 0
agree 8 of 8, accepted 3
whole
bounded
Core\Uuid::parse(): "f9168c5e-ceb2-4faa-b6bf-329bf39fa1e" is not a UUID — the canonical form is eight hexadecimal digits, three groups of four and then twelve, hyphen-separated, as in "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000"
